<!-- WHY VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Why Visit Chongqing</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127983;</div><h3>Cyberpunk Cityscape</h3><p>Wander a city where trains run through buildings, highways stack six layers high, and Hongya Cave's cascading lights create a real-life Blade Runner backdrop — Chongqing is unlike any city on earth.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127754;</div><h3>Three Gorges Majesty</h3><p>Embark on a Yangtze River cruise through the legendary Three Gorges — Qutang, Wu, and Xiling — where sheer cliffs rise thousands of feet from the water along one of the world's great river journeys.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127798;</div><h3>Sichuan Hot Pot</h3><p>Chongqing is the undisputed capital of mala (numbing-spicy) cuisine. The city's legendary hot pot — bubbling cauldrons of chili oil and Sichuan peppercorns — is a gastronomic rite of passage.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9961;</div><h3>UNESCO Treasures</h3><p>Discover the Dazu Rock Carvings — over 50,000 Buddhist, Taoist, and Confucian figures carved into cliffs between the 9th and 13th centuries — and the dramatic karst landscapes of Wulong.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TOP ATTRACTIONS -->
<section class="section section-alt"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Top Attractions</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="tours-grid">
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Hongya Cave" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#1</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Hongya Cave (Hongyadong)</h3><p>An 11-story stilt-house complex clinging to a cliff above the Jialing River that transforms into a glittering spectacle at night. The UNESCO-listed architecture recreates old Chongqing — explore handicraft shops, sample local snacks, and find the perfect rooftop bar for panoramic river views.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Three Gorges" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#2</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Three Gorges &amp; Yangtze River Cruise</h3><p>Board a luxury cruiser in Chongqing for a 3-4 day voyage through the Three Gorges — the most dramatic stretch of the Yangtze. Highlights include the narrow Qutang Gorge, the ethereal Wu Gorge shrouded in mist, and the massive Three Gorges Dam. Shore excursions to Fengdu Ghost City and Shennong Stream add cultural depth.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Ciqikou Ancient Town" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#3</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Ciqikou Ancient Town (Porcelain Village)</h3><p>A 1,700-year-old river port town with narrow flagstone lanes, traditional teahouses, and artisan workshops. Once a major porcelain production center (hence the name), today it's a charming tangle of snack stalls, folk music performances, and calligraphy shops. Try the famous Chen Mahua (fried dough twists).</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Dazu Rock Carvings" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#4</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Dazu Rock Carvings</h3><p>A UNESCO World Heritage site featuring some of China's finest religious rock art. Unlike earlier grottoes, Dazu's carvings blend Buddhist, Taoist, and Confucian themes in vivid, narrative detail. The Beishan and Baodingshan sites showcase thousands of figures, with the 31-meter reclining Buddha being the undisputed highlight.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Wulong Karst" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#5</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Wulong Karst National Park</h3><p>Another UNESCO site, famous for its Three Natural Bridges — massive limestone arches spanning dramatic gorges. The karst landscape of sinkholes, caves, and forest-covered canyons served as a filming location for Transformers: Age of Extinction. The glass observation deck at Longshuixia Fissure overlooks a 200m-deep gorge.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- BEST TIME TO VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127800;</div><h3>Spring (Mar-May)</h3><p>Mild and pleasant (15-25&deg;C) with occasional rain. The city's parks burst with cherry blossoms and magnolias. March-April is a great time for Three Gorges cruises before the summer heat and crowds arrive.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127774;</div><h3>Autumn (Sep-Nov)</h3><p>The best season — comfortable temperatures (15-25&deg;C) and the famous "Chongqing fog" burns off by mid-morning. The mountain scenery around Wulong and Dazu is spectacular with autumn colors. October has the most pleasant weather for city exploration.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9924;</div><h3>Summer (Jun-Aug)</h3><p>Infamously hot and humid (30-40&deg;C) — Chongqing is one of China's "Three Furnaces." The city comes alive at night. Hongya Cave's night views are most magical. Three Gorges cruises offer refreshing breezes on deck. Start sightseeing early and embrace the evening culture.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#10052;</div><h3>Winter (Dec-Feb)</h3><p>Cool and damp (5-10&deg;C) with heavy fog — which is actually part of Chongqing's mystical charm. Perfect hot pot weather! The mountain landscapes take on a mysterious, mist-shrouded quality. Fewer tourists and lower prices. Dazu Rock Carvings are particularly atmospheric in winter light.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

